## Why This Is Asked
This question tests mastery of advanced data structure design by combining segment trees with multisets to handle weighted intervals with duplicate support. It also evaluates understanding of binary search applied to aggregated data structures for kth largest queries.

## Key Concepts
- **Coordinate compression** on endpoints to discretize the domain
- **Segment tree** where each node stores a multiset of weights for intervals covering that node's range
- **Interval decomposition** into O(log n) nodes for efficient updates
- **Path aggregation** for point sum queries
- **Binary search over weight values** with counts from multisets to support kthLargest queries

## Code Example
```javascript
class IntervalDS {
  constructor(coords) {
    this.coords = coords;
    this.N = coords.length;
    this.tree = Array(4 * this.N).fill(null).map(() => new Map());
    this.sums = Array(4 * this.N).fill(0);
  }

  insert(l, r, w) {
    this.update(1, 0, this.N - 1, l, r, w, 1);
  }

  erase(l, r, w) {
    this.update(1, 0, this.N - 1, l, r, w, -1);
  }

  update(node, start, end, l, r, w, delta) {
    if (r < start || end < l) return;
    if (l <= start && end <= r) {
      const count = this.tree[node].get(w) || 0;
      this.tree[node].set(w, count + delta);
      if (this.tree[node].get(w) === 0) this.tree[node].delete(w);
      this.sums[node] += w * delta;
      return;
    }
    const mid = Math.floor((start + end) / 2);
    this.update(node * 2, start, mid, l, r, w, delta);
    this.update(node * 2 + 1, mid + 1, end, l, r, w, delta);
    this.sums[node] = this.sums[node * 2] + this.sums[node * 2 + 1];
  }

  sumAtPoint(x) {
    return this.querySum(1, 0, this.N - 1, x);
  }

  querySum(node, start, end, x) {
    if (x < start || x > end) return 0;
    if (start === end) return this.sums[node];
    const mid = Math.floor((start + end) / 2);
    return this.sums[node] + 
      (x <= mid ? this.querySum(node * 2, start, mid, x) 
                : this.querySum(node * 2 + 1, mid + 1, end, x));
  }

  kthLargest(x, k) {
    const weights = [...new Set(this.getWeightsAtPoint(x))].sort((a, b) => b - a);
    if (k > weights.length) return null;
    return weights[k - 1];
  }

  getWeightsAtPoint(x) {
    const result = [];
    this.collectWeights(1, 0, this.N - 1, x, result);
    return result;
  }

  collectWeights(node, start, end, x, result) {
    if (x < start || x > end) return;
    for (const [w, count] of this.tree[node]) {
      result.push(...Array(count).fill(w));
    }
    if (start === end) return;
    const mid = Math.floor((start + end) / 2);
    this.collectWeights(node * 2, start, mid, x, result);
    this.collectWeights(node * 2 + 1, mid + 1, end, x, result);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to implement a compact ranking structure with duplicates and a fixed value range, using O(log U) updates and O(log U) kth-order queries.

## Key Concepts

- Fenwick tree for prefix sums
- Binary lifting to find k-th order statistic (standard BIT trick: find largest power of 2 <= n, then iterate downward checking cumulative tree values)
- Handling duplicates via per-value counts

## Code Example

```javascript
class Fenwick {
  constructor(n) { 
    this.n = n; 
    this.tree = new Array(n + 1).fill(0); 
  }

  add(i, delta) {
    for (; i <= this.n; i += i & -i) this.tree[i] += delta;
  }

  sum(i) {
    let s = 0;
    for (; i > 0; i -= i & -i) s += this.tree[i];
    return s;
  }

  kth(k) {
    if (k < 1) return -1;
    const total = this.sum(this.n);
    if (k > total) return -1;

    let idx = 0;
    let bit = 1;
    while (bit << 1 <= this.n) bit <<= 1;

    let sum = 0;
    for (; bit > 0; bit >>= 1) {
      const next = idx + bit;
      if (next <= this.n && sum + this.tree[next] < k) {
        sum += this.tree[next];
        idx = next;
      }
    }
    return idx; // 0-indexed
  }
}

class Leaderboard {
  constructor(maxScore = 1000000) {
    this.bit = new Fenwick(maxScore + 1);
  }

  insertScore(s) { this.bit.add(s + 1, 1); }

  eraseScore(s) { this.bit.add(s + 1, -1); }

  getKthScore(k) { return this.bit.kth(k) - 1; }

  getRank(s) { return this.bit.sum(s + 1); }
}
```

## Handling Ties

Since each score has its own count, duplicates naturally work:
- insertScore(50) adds count at index 50
- insertScore(50) again increments same index
- eraseScore(50) decrements it
- getRank(50) returns all scores <= 50 including duplicates
- getKthScore(k) skips to the k-th element accounting for counts

## Why This Is Asked

Evaluates ability to implement streaming statistics with efficient data structures and real-time median calculation.

## Key Concepts

- Dual heap structure (max-heap + min-heap)
- Balance invariant maintenance
- Median computation in O(log n) per insert
- Streaming data processing

## Code Example

```javascript
class MedianStream {
  constructor() {
    this.low = new MaxHeap();  // Lower half
    this.high = new MinHeap(); // Upper half
  }

  insert(x) {
    // Insert into appropriate heap and rebalance
    if (this.low.isEmpty() || x <= this.low.peek()) {
      this.low.push(x);
    } else {
      this.high.push(x);
    }

    // Rebalance to maintain size difference ≤ 1
    if (this.low.size() > this.high.size() + 1) {
      this.high.push(this.low.pop());
    } else if (this.high.size() > this.low.size() + 1) {
      this.low.push(this.high.pop());
    }
  }

  getMedian() {
    if (this.low.size() > this.high.size()) {
      return this.low.peek();
    } else if (this.high.size() > this.low.size()) {
      return this.high.peek();
    } else {
      return (this.low.peek() + this.high.peek()) / 2;
    }
  }
}
```

